**What was wrong?**

| Problem | Why it fails |
|---------|--------------|
| `document.getAnimations("t3")` | There is no method called `getAnimations`. You meant **`getElementById`**. |
| Variable names don’t match | Inside the `if / else` blocks you wrote `num1`, `num2`, `num3` but the variables were declared as `n1`, `n2`, `n3`. |
| Quotes mixed up | The inner‑HTML assignments used double quotes inside a double‑quoted string – it works but is confusing. |
| No handling of “none of them is larger” | The last `else` always prints `num3` even when `n1` or `n2` could be larger. |

Below is a **cleaned‑up version** of the script that fixes those issues and makes the logic straightforward.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Largest Number</title>
</head>
<body>

<p>Write a program to find the largest number among 3 numbers.</p>

<input type="text" placeholder="Enter 1st number" id="t1">
<br>
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter 2nd number" id="t2">
<br>
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter 3rd number" id="t3">
<button onclick="check()">Check</button>
<h3 id="ans">The largest number is</h3>

<script>
function check() {
    // Get the values from the input fields
    let n1 = document.getElementById('t1').value;
    let n2 = document.getElementById('t2').value;
    let n3 = document.getElementById('t3').value;

    // Convert to numbers (parseFloat will turn "abc" into NaN)
    let num1 = parseFloat(n1);
    let num2 = parseFloat(n2);
    let num3 = parseFloat(n3);

    // Simple comparison – works for both positive & negative numbers
    if (num1 >= num2 && num1 >= num3) {
        document.getElementById('ans').innerHTML = "The largest is " + num1;
    } else if (num2 >= num1 && num2 >= num3) {
        document.getElementById('ans').innerHTML = "The largest is " + num2;
    } else {
        document.getElementById('ans').innerHTML = "The largest is " + num3;
    }
}
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### What changed?

1. **`document.getElementById('t3')`** – replaced the non‑existent `getAnimations`.
2. **Consistent variable names** – now `n1`, `n2`, `n3` are stored exactly as later referenced.
3. **Proper conversion to numbers** – `parseFloat()` turns the text input into a real number; you can add extra checks if you want to guard against non‑numeric entries.
4. **Fixed innerHTML assignments** – removed stray commas and made the string literals consistent.
5. **Simplified the logic** – the three mutually exclusive `if / else if / else` branches cover every possible case.

Copy the whole block above into an `.html` file and open it in a browser. When you enter three numbers and click **Check**, the `<h3>` will display the largest one.

